It is clear that the desired statement is equivalent to one square bearing the number 3.

Now we are in business. There are  $mn-1$  edges in the path; each one not on the perimeter contributes to two squares. There are  $2m + 2n - 4$  perimeter edges, but not all can be used.

Hence at most  $2m + 2n - 5$  edges are single-counted, and at least  $mn - 2m - 2n + 4$  are double-counted. Thus the total contribution is at least
\[2(mn - 2m - 2n + 4) + (2m + 2n - 5) = 2mn - 2m - 2n + 3.\]
This exceeds twice the number of squares; hence some square bears the number 3.

