Trolling problem  :wallbash: . Getting 47 is pretty easy, but I've spent about 2 hours trying to find a construction until I find a construction to 46 and then prove that 47 is in fact impossible(which practically the same thing). 

For the bounding part I'll solve the following above mentioned graph theory interpretation: <blockquote> What is the maximum number of edges in a subgraph of  $K_{10,10}$  which is  $K_{2,3}$  free?</blockquote>

Let the parts of the subgraph  $G$  be  $A$  and  $B$ . Lets double-count the number   $$ N := |\{(b,(a_i,a_j)); \text{where  $b$  is a vertex of  $B$  which is adjacent to both vertices  $a_i$  and  $a_j$  in  $A$ }\}| $$   

Counting by the number of pairs  $(a_i,a_j)$  we know that,   $$ |N(a_i)\cap N(a_j)| \leq 2 $$   hence   $$ N=\sum_{i\neq j} |N(a_i)\cap N(a_j)| \leq 2 \binom{10}{2}=90 $$   But, counting by the  $b_i$ s   $$ 90\geq N=\sum_i \binom{d(b_i)}2 $$   Now, note that, by  $QM\geq AM$    $$ \frac{(\sum_i d(b_i))^2}{10}\leq \sum_i d(b_i)^2 $$     $$ 90\geq \frac{d(b_i)}2 \geq \frac{\frac{(\sum_i d(b_i))^2}{10} - \sum_i d(b_i)}2 $$   Now, if  $S=\sum_i d(b_i)$    $$ 0 \geq S^2-10S -1800 \Rightarrow \boxed{S\leq 47} $$  And, since we wish to maximize the number  $E=\sum_i d(b_i)$  and  $\binom{x}2$  is clearly convex, by Jensen's we get that this maximum is achieved when all  $b_i$ s are close to each others and one can see the only case we get  $S=47$  is when we have the following distribuition of degrees  $(5,5,5,5,5,5,5,4,4,4,4)$ .

Now suppose FTSoC there exists such graph with  $47$  edges. Consider the subgraph  $G'=((A,B'),E))$  of  $G$  where  $B'$  is the set of the  $7$  vertices with have degree  $5$ . Now, we repeat the same double-counting on  $G'$ :   $$ \sum_{i\neq j} |N(b'_i)\cap N(b'_j)| = \sum_i \binom{d(a_i)}{2} \leq 2\binom{7}2 $$   but, on the other hand, we have  $\sum_i d(a_i)=35$  and  $|A|=10$  and thus by  $QM\geq AM$  again, we get   $$ \sum_i \binom{d(a_i)}2 \geq 10\binom{\frac{35}{10}}2 >42 $$   contradiction!

