<details><summary>Just some remaks and my opinion about this problem.</summary>I think that this is not a good problem  for a 6 on a national olympiad. In fact the double-counting argument is nice, but it's one of the most well-known techniques an experienced solver would try for a global problem like this one. As I mentioned and as most of you probably noticed, the hardest part is to think about improving the bound, which just repeats the same idea! In short, the problem itself is quite good, but not that good for a problem 6. One could easily check that the double counting argument we did would achieve the bound  $\lfloor \frac{n(1+\sqrt{8n-7})}2 \rfloor$  by substituting  $10$  for  $n$ . Thus, replace  $10$  with a number of the form  $\frac{m^2+7}8$ (like 11, for example), with  $m$  being odd, would make it a really nice Problem 2(maybe a p1(?)) where the bound will be strict and a little generalization of the construction above will work.</details>

stem_md:
Show an infinite sequence  $a_1, a_2, \ldots$  of integers with both of the following properties:

•  $a_i \neq 0$  for every positive integer  $i$ , that is, no term in the sequence is equal to zero;

• for all positive integer  $n$ ,  $a_n + a_{2n} + \ldots + a_{2023n} = 0$ .

solution_md:
Let  $U$  and  $V$  be points such that  $\Delta ABU$  and  $\Delta BCV$  are equilateral triangles and  $\angle CBU=\angle VBA=150^{\circ}$ . Let  $Q=AV\cap CU$ .

[asy]
import geometry;
size(10cm);
pair C=dir(180);
pair B=dir(75);
pair A=dir(0);
triangle t=triangle(A,B,C);
pair T=intersectionpoint(perpendicular(B,line(A,C)),line(A,C));
pair X_=B+rotate(60)*(T-B);
pair Y_=B+rotate(-60)*(T-B);
pair U=B+rotate(60)*(A-B);
pair V=B+rotate(-60)*(C-B);
pair P=intersectionpoint(line(A,Y_),line(C,X_));
pair Q=intersectionpoint(line(A,V),line(C,U));
filldraw(arc(Q,V,B,0.25)--V--cycle,palered,palered);
filldraw(arc(T,C,P,0.25)--C--cycle,palered,palered);
filldraw(arc(Q,C,B,0.25)--C--cycle,palered,palered);
filldraw(arc(B,U,Q,0.25)--U--cycle,paleblue,paleblue);
filldraw(arc(B,A,Q,0.25)--A--cycle,paleblue,paleblue);
filldraw(arc(P,A,C,0.25)--A--cycle,paleblue,paleblue);
draw(t);
draw(circumcircle(A,B,U),dotted); draw(circumcircle(C,B,V),dotted);
draw(B--X_--T--Y_--B); draw(C--V--B--U--A);
draw(Y_--A); draw(X_--C);
draw(A--V,dotted); draw(C--U,dotted);
draw(B--T);
dot(" $A$ ",A,(1,-1));
dot(" $B$ ",B,(0.25,1.25));
dot(" $C$ ",C,(-1,-1));
dot(" $T$ ",T,(1,-1));
dot(" $X$ ",X_,(0.5,1));
dot(" $Y$ ",Y_,(-0.25,1));
dot(" $U$ ",U,(0.5,0.5));
dot(" $V$ ",V,(-0.25,0.5));
dot(" $P$ ",P,(0.5,1));
dot(" $Q$ ",Q,(-1,0.75));
[/asy]

First, we note that  $Q$  is the Fermat point of triangle  $\Delta ABC$ , and it's well known that quadrilaterals  $AQBU$  and  $CQBV$  are cyclic. Thus,  $\angle BUQ=\angle BAQ$  and  $\angle QCB=\angle QVB$ .
Note that  $\tfrac{AB}{BC}=\tfrac{BT}{TC}$   $\implies$   $\tfrac{BU}{BC}=\tfrac{XT}{TC}$  and since  $\angle CBU=\angle XTC=90^{\circ}+60^{\circ}=150^{\circ}$  we have  $\Delta CBU\sim \Delta XTC$   $\implies$   $\angle UCB=\angle TCX$ . Analogously, we obtain that  $\angle BAV=\angle YAT$ . In other words,  $PA$ ,  $QA$  are isogonals on angle  $\angle BAC$  and  $PC$ ,  $QC$  are isogonals on angle  $\angle ACB$ .
Now, it is clear that  $\Delta PAC \sim \Delta BUC$   $(\angle PAC=\angle BUC; \angle UCB=\angle ACP)$  and so  $\tfrac{PC}{CA}=\tfrac{BC}{CU}$ , but since  $\angle PCB=\angle ACU$  (because  $PC$ ,  $QC$  are isogonals on  $\angle ACB$ ) it follows that  $\Delta PCB \sim \Delta ACU$ . The last similarity implies that  $\tfrac{PC}{PB}=\tfrac{CA}{AU}=\tfrac{CA}{AB}$  and so  $PC\cdot AB=PB\cdot CA$ .
Analogously, we obtain that  $PA\cdot BC=PB\cdot CA$  and the problem is done.

stem_md:
Let \( n > 1 \) be a positive integer. List in increasing order all the irreducible fractions in the interval \([0, 1]\) that have a positive denominator less than or equal to \( n \):

\[
\frac{0}{1}
=
\frac{p_0}{q_0}
<
\frac{p_1}{q_1}
< \cdots <
\frac{p_M}{q_M}
=
\frac{1}{1}.
\]

Determine, in function of \( n \), the smallest possible value of \( q_{i-1} + q_i + q_{i+1} \), for \( 0 < i < M \).

For example, if \( n = 4 \), the enumeration is 
\[
\frac{0}{1}
<
\frac{1}{4}
<
\frac{1}{3}
<
\frac{1}{2}
<
\frac{2}{3}
<
\frac{3}{4}
<
\frac{1}{1},
\]
where \( p_0 = 0, p_1 = 1, p_2 = 1, p_3 = 1, p_4 = 2, p_5 = 3, p_6 = 1, q_0 = 1, q_1 = 4, q_2 = 3, q_3 = 2, q_4 = 3, q_5 = 4, q_6 = 1 \), and the minimum is \( 1 + 4 + 3 = 3 + 2 + 3 = 3 + 4 + 1 = 8 \).

solution_md:
We claim that the smallest size of a blocking set in a polygon with  $n$  vertices is  $n-2$ . Let's prove the claim:

Let  $A_i$  be the  $i$ -th vertex of the polygon in clockwise order (consider all indices modulo  $n$ ). We have the following construction of a blocking set  $X_n$  with  $n-2$  diagonals:
\[X_n=\{A_{i}A_{i+2}\ |\ 1\leq i\leq n-2\}\]
[asy]import geometry;
size(10cm);
pair A[];
for(int n=6;n<9;n+=1){
  for(int i=0;i<n;i+=1)A[i]=dir(90-180/n-360*i/n)+(n*3.5,0);
  //construction
  draw(A[0]--A[n-2],lightgray);
  draw(A[1]--A[n-1],lightgray);
  for(int i=0;i<n-2;i+=1){
    draw(A[i]--A[(i+2)%n],red);
  }
  //polygon
  for(int i=0;i<n;i+=1){
    dot(" $A_{"+string(i+1)+"}$ ",A[i],dir(90-180/n-360*i/n));
    draw(A[i]--A[(i+1)%n]);
  }
  label(" $X_{"+string(n)+"}$ ",(n*3.5,0));
}
[/asy]The construction is valid for  $n=4$  and it is not hard to prove that any triangulation of a polygon with  $n\geq 5$  vertices must have  $2$  or more diagonals  $A_{i}A_{i+2}$  (use strong induction: it is clearly valid for  $n=5$ , and the induction step consists in the fact that any diagonal divides the polygon into two smaller polygons). It follows that any triangulation of a polygon with  $n$  vertices will include at least  $1$  diagonal  $A_iA_{i+2}$  from  $X_n$  (note that a triangulation cannot include  $A_{n-1}A_1$  and  $A_{n}A_2$  at the same time), which implies that  $X_n$  is, in fact, a blocking set.

Now, we prove, by induction, that we cannot have a blocking set in a polygon with  $n$  vertices with less than  $n-2$  diagonals:
FTSOC, assume that the minimum number of diagonals of a blocking set is  $n-2$  for a polygon with  $n\leq n_ 0$  vertices and we have a blocking set with less than  $n_0-1$  diagonals in a polygon with  $n_0+1$  vertices.
We use the fact that we always have a diagonal  $A_iA_{i+2}$  not included in the blocking set crossing a diagonal included in the blocking set: It is not hard to see that if we try to construct a blocking set without this property we will eventually include all the diagonals  $A_iA_{i+2}$ , which implies this set must have more than  $n_0$  diagonals. (see the following diagram: if we start with any diagonal in the blocking set, we will eventually include all the diagonals  $A_iA_{i+2}$ )
[asy]import geometry;
size(14cm);
int n=10;
pair A[];
for(int cnt=0;cnt<3;cnt+=1){
  for(int i=0;i<n;i+=1)A[i]=dir(180-180/n-360*i/n)+(cnt*4,0);
  draw(A[1]--(cnt*4,0),red+dotted);
  for(int i=0;i<cnt+1;i+=1){
    draw(A[i]--A[(i+2)%n],red);
  }
  //polygon
  dot(" $A_{i}$ ",A[0],dir(180-180/n));
  for(int i=1;i<n/2;i+=1){
    dot(" $A_{i+"+string(i)+"}$ ",A[i],dir(180-180/n-360*i/n));
    draw(A[i]--A[(i-1)%n]);
  }
}
[/asy]Thus, we can start the triangulation of the polygon with  $n_0+1$  vertices using some diagonal  $A_iA_{i+2}$  that is not included in the blocking set but crosses at least one diagonal from the blocking set. This operation creates a new polygon with  $n_0$  vertices and reduces the number of diagonals from the blocking set to less than  $n_0-2$  (note that at least one diagonal from the original blocking set crosses the initial diagonal  $A_iA_{i+2}$ , and this diagonal is not included in the smaller polygon). This is a contradiction because the minimum number of diagonals of a blocking set in a polygon with  $n_0$  vertices is  $n_0-2$  (by our assumption). Since this holds for the case  $n=4$ , the induction is valid: We cannot have a blocking set with less than  $n-2$  diagonals in a polygon with  $n$  vertices.

