Let us focus on the bound. Assign the coordinate  $(x,y)$  to each cell where  $1\leq x,y\leq n$ . Note that the sum counts the number of triples  $(x,y,z)$  such that the cell  $(x,y)$  is white and the cell  $(y,z)$  is black. Call such triples *fantastic*. The key observation is the following.


- If  $(x,y,z)$  is fantastic, then its cyclic shifts  $(y,z,x)$  and  $(z,x,y)$  are both not fantastic. This is clear from the definition.
- $(x,x,x)$  is not fantastic for each  $1\leq x\leq n$ .

There are  $n^3-n$  triples whose not all entries are equal, each of which has exactly three different cyclic shifts; at most one cyclic shift can be fantastic. Thus, there are at most  $\tfrac{n^3-n}{3}$  fantastic triples, so we are done.</details>

