This is a really boring problem in the sense that one has to try many cases before getting something.
The answer is that such sequence exists. A colleague of mine got the explicit construction, but I don´t remember it.
I will try then to show why such sequence must exist.

Allow us to have the following construction: $i$ )  $a \neq 0$  and  $b = 0$  so that  $x_{n} = a^{n-1}$  for all  $n \geq 1$ .  $ii$ )  $x^{2} - cx - d = 0$  has complex conjugate roots  $\beta$  and  $\overline{\beta}$  so that  $y_{n} = \frac{1}{\beta - \overline{\beta}} \left( \beta^{n} - (\overline{\beta})^{n}\right)$ . $iii$ ) We also impose that  $|a| = |\beta|$ .

Then, we have:
\begin{align*}
\frac{|x_{n}|}{|y_{n}|} &= \frac{|\beta - \overline{\beta}|}{|a|} \frac{|a|^{n}}{|\beta^{n} - \overline{\beta}^{n}|}
&= \frac{|\beta||2i\sin(\theta)|}{|a|} \frac{|a|^{n}}{|\beta|^{n}|2i\sin(n\theta)|}
& = \frac{|sin(\theta)|}{|\sin(n\theta)|}
\end{align*}

Note that, if  $\sin(n\theta)$  gets arbitrarily close to 0 and 1, we are done, as we would have  $\frac{|x_{n}|}{|y_{n}|}$  getting arbitrarily big and close to 1, respectively.

Then you just pick a  $\theta$  such that  $\sin(n\theta)$  is dense and make such you can find a polynomial of degree two with integer coeffiecients such that $\arg(\beta) = \theta)$ . I'm too tired to work it out in details, but I'm gonna do it later.

For letter (a), note that, as there is a  $M > 0$  such that  $\frac{n_{k+1}}{n_{k}} < M$  for all  $k \geq 1$ , we have:

\begin{align*}
\sum_{n=1}^{n_{k+1}-1} \frac{a_{n}}{n}  &= \sum_{i=1}^{k} \sum_{n_{i} \leq n < n_{i+1} } \frac{ a_{n} }{n} 
& < \sum_{i=1}^{k} ( n_{i+1} - n_{i} -1) \frac{ a_{n_{i}} }{n_{i}} 
& < \sum_{i=1}^{k} \frac{ n_{i+1} }{ n_{i} }  a_{n_{i}} 
&  < M  \sum_{i=1}^{k} a_{n_{i}}
\end{align*}

Hence, we have that
\[
\sum_{k \geq 1} a_{n_{k}} > \frac{1}{M} \sum_{n \geq 1} \frac{a_{n}}{n}
\]
And so  $\sum_{k \geq 1} a_{n_{k}}$  diverges.

For (b), in a similar fashion, one can find a subsequence such that  $\frac{n_{k+1}}{n_{k}} \to 1 + \epsilon$  for any  $\epsilon > 0$  and  $\sum_{k \geq 1} a_{n_{k}}$  converges, by using that  $\sum_{i=n_{k}}^{n_{k+1}-1} \frac{1}{n} \sim \log \left(\frac{n_{k+1}-1}{n_{k}-1} \right)$ .

However, I couldn´t prove the proposition in its full extent. Bump for (b)?

stem_md:
Metadieu, Tercieu and Quartieu are three bodybuilder warriors who fight against an  $n$ -headed monster. Each of them can attack the monster according to the following rules:

(1) Metadieu's attack consists of cutting off half of the monster's heads, then cutting off one more head. If the monster's number of heads is odd, Metadieu cannot attack;

(2) Tercieu's attack consists of cutting off a third of the monster's heads, then cutting off two more heads. If the monster's number of heads is not a multiple of 3, Tercieu cannot attack;

(3) Quartieu's attack consists of cutting off a quarter of the monster's heads, then cutting off three more heads. If the monster's number of heads is not a multiple of 4, Quartieu cannot attack;

If none of the three warriors can attack the monster at some point, then it will devour our three heroes. The objective of the three warriors is to defeat the monster, and for that they need to cut off all its heads, one warrior attacking at a time.

For what positive integer values of  $n$  is it possible for the three warriors to combine a sequence of attacks in order to defeat the monster?

