The function  $f:\mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$  is such that  $f(x+y) \geq f(x) + yf(f(x))$  for all  $x,y \in \mathbb{R}$ . Prove that:

a)  $f(f(x)) \leq 0$  for all  $x \in \mathbb{R}$ ;
b) if  $f(0) \geq 0$ , then  $f(x) = 0$  for all  $x\in \mathbb{R}$

candidates:
@above you firstly say that any two integers with difference  $1937$  must be in the same group but immediately afterwards state that  $y$  and  $y+1937$  are in different groups, how does this happen??

<details><summary>False, since the three with the same colour could be different for different n</summary>Sketch: Let  $a=50$ ,  $b=1987$ . By the problem condition, for any  $n$  the numbers in a triple  $(n, n-a, n+b)$ ,  $(n, n+a, n+a+b)$ ,  $(n, n-b, n-a-b)$  have different colours, so  $n-a$ ,  $n+a$ ,  $n+b$ ,  $n-b$ ,  $n-a-b$ ,  $n+a+b$  all have different colours from  $n$ . But there are two available colours when we exclude that of  $n$ , so some three among these six numbers must have the same colour. On the other hand, if it is the case that any two numbers with difference  $d_i$ ,  $i=1,2$ , have the same colour, then the same holds for numbers with difference  $\gcd(d_1,d_2)$  by Bezout's lemma.
A quick case bash on the possible differences of the above  $6$  numbers gives that always  $n$  and  $n+1$  must be in the same colour for all  $n$ , contradiction.</details>

